Subject: UPLOADING MINDS: Next Step

Bruce: 
> I'm serious. I'd expect at least 1 comment from the readers of this list
> to my last post.

For newbies to get a response, you must post flame bait like
good ol' Jeff F.  (One reason we get so many "newbie perversions"!)

> You all make the assumption that the mind-uploading technology will somehow
> just be there without 1990's style Internet or phone or whatever lobbying
> on our part to make it happen. 

And here it is!  :-)

Not "somehow just be there".  Like most technology, it will likely
emerge from a long line of projects using related technology for
different purposes.  The most critical uploading tech, neuron 
simulation, will likely emerge from neuro R&D in the
biotech community.  One of the many near-term applications
of this kind of knowledge is replacing damaged neurons (eg
for Alzheimer's and Parkinson's).  Uploading, cell repair,
nanotech, etc. won't come from a grandiose long-term plans, lobbying 
for government projects, Superconducting Super Uploaders, ad nauseum.
Long-range tech will come from a long line of skilled engineers 
solving feasible short term problems, standing on the shoulders of 
those who solved problems, and providing shoulders for those who 
solve the later ones.

Some communities related to the Extropian community (eg the National
Space Society, the Assembler Multitude, etc.) have a very engrained
habit of jumping from "this is an important technology" to "we must start
planning/working on it now" and the corollary "greedy short-term
business isn't buying so we need to lobby for government funding".
Extropians recognize that very long-range future tech can be critically
important to how we live our lives today, without being important at all 
to the motivations of either government or business, or our own immediate
welfare.  An example is cryonics cell repair.  The probability of this 
tech becoming feasible within the next few centuries is critically 
important to the feasbility of cryonics,  but the lack of current 
feasbility is not.  The only market reason to fund cell repair 
research today is out of cryonics marketing budgets, and for the sole 
purpose of demonstrating that it will likely one day be possible (eg with
advanced nanotech), not for the purpose of implementing in any
reasonable business timeline (biotech goes out about 10-15 years).
This is a very different kind of research, theoretical applied
science, which is unfortuneately quite lacking in our current
R&D culture.  Another broad area we Extropians should work on
changing, after making inroads in medical ethics.  But our
expectations for funding should be quite modest, main sources
being future cryonics marketing and miscellaneous "blue sky" R&D 
groups.  _Not_ startups, _not_ people intending to actually
implement the technology.

There isn't thing one we can or should do about uploading right
now, beyond (a) determining how probable it is, (b) roughly how
much of an improvement it could bring about (and potential
downsides) and (c) what we can do for ourselves to make sure we 
get that far (and indeed if it's worthwhile sacrificing for now to 
get a probable enormous payoff later).  Things
we can do now including basic life extension (taking care of our
health), signing up for cryonics, raising Extropian children, teaching,
Extropian ethics, making money to do all those things, training and 
working so that we can make that money, etc.  Reducing entropy is a 
daily task.  Those who solve the problems at hand today will
be the ones best prepared when it comes time to upload.
Those who spend their days whining at the world to make their long-term
dreams come true will be six feet under, "Preterites".

> Most of our allies in medicine
> need to be found, nurtured, highlighted.
> The next latest and greatest
> computer alone is not going to cut it. 

This is a very important point, in a slightly different
context -- bringing Extropian ethics to the medical community
and creating biotech organizations pursuing enhancement goals (smart
drugs, life extension, cryonics, etc.) as well as cure-sickness
goals (the current dominant medical paradigm).  The current
ethic that only trivial characteristics should be enhanced 
(eg cosmetic surgery), but that important characteristics should
be left as is (intelligence, lifespan, etc). is morally bankrupt, 
but there aren't any Extropian medical ethicists to get the word
out and balance the direction of research, which is obsessed with
repair over prevention, and curing the sick over enhancing the well.

