Subject: selection for literacy

Whether there are "literacy genes", and whether there was pressure
in favor of such genes in previous centuries, the pressure is quite
the opposite now.  Across a wide variety of cultures, 
alleles of illiterate people are spreading at nearly twice the
rate of the alleles of literates.   Illiterates are genetically
fitter than literates, and people with PhDs who use birth control
are the least fit of all.  

The doubling time of a hypothetical perfectly correlating "illiteracy" 
vs. a "literacy" allele is about 25 years.  Illiterates have their 
children at a younger age, in addition to having more of them.  If 
currently 10% of the world population carry such the illiterate gene, 
then in only 150 years 92% of the population will be carrying it.  Yes 
Virginia, evolution can and does work over short timeframes when 
there is that kind of selection pressure.   Cf. animal breeding.
Of course, there are probably no such genes with 100% correlation 
to literacy.  A more reasonable scenario: if twice as many illiterates 
as literates carry a allele, then it takes 260 years to go from 10%
to 92%.

It's critical to study the actual demographics instead of speculating
about what is "fit" and what isn't.  For starters, try this month's 
_Scientific American_ and pre-1987 World Almanacs for education vs. 
fertility charts.  Then try a good social science library.
"Common sense" and the accepted wisdom in these areas 
are often woefully wrong.  Be prepared for some rude shocks.

