Date: Tue, 21 Dec 1993 01:14:41 -0800 (PST)
From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: Vinge's Technological Singularity

Robin Hanson: 
> But I see designing intelligence as harder than designing hardware,

Furthermore, designing intelligence is far more uncertain than 
extrapolating improvements in hardware (either
via trendline or via theoretical designs, eg Drexler's nanomachines).
If we can predict the precise effects of a piece of software, we've
already written it.  Predicting and writing software are nearly
the same thing.

In general, some kinds of tech are far more predictable than others.
Thus, I don't buy "The" Singularity.  There are uncountably many
singularities, depending on what aspect of the future we are trying
to predict.  This is true for even a narrow subset of the future, eg
the weather.  It's easier to predict tommorrow's weather than
next week's.   We can often predict tommorrow's temperature more 
accurately than tommorrow's precipitation.  We can predict next
year's average temperature with greater accuracy than next Monday's
average temperature.  At some point all these variables become
unpredictable enough we might effectively say they reach a 
"singularity" -- for daily vs. historical daily temperature this
might be as little as a few days, for yearly average absolute
this might be on the order of billions of years! (sans tech
intervention in the climate).  Some variables return to home
base, some zoom off into a singularity.

Tech goes in leaps and bounds, quick learning curve climbings
followed by stasis.  Puncuated equilibrium.  So it's often
hard to say what's more predictable.  Most basic transportation
technology was known by 1940, and more progress was made by 1960, but
not much new since then; transport tech has gone into near stasis, which
might last quite a while.  Right now computer and
communications are the stars.  Computer progress may slow down in 
the near term (silicon fab limits) and speed up again in the long 
term (atomic-scale circuitry).  Some software tech nearly as
slow as rocket tech (COBOL, FORTRAN invented around same time
as Atlas and Titan rockets), but more punctuated (spreadsheets
invented and in widespread use within a span of two years).
If nanoscale self-replication/flexible manufacturing is easy -- a 
major unknown, almost as mysterious as AI -- space-based industry 
might open up vastly cheaper energy, real estate
etc., breaking the current stasis in those areas (and transport,
at least interplanetary).

Vinge is relying on the least predictable technology of all, the
computerization of intelligence, to predict "The" Singularity.
If somebody could quantify human intelligence, eg "the ability
to do arithmetic constituted X% of human intelligence at baseline
date 19XX", we could start extrapolating what's been automated
so far, maybe the curve could be fit well.    But I haven't
seen that sort of thing.

> And if
> superhumans come by amplifying ordinary intelligence, I don't see why
> most folks couldn't remain productive.

They might remain productive in the same way hunter-gatherer tribes
have remained productive.  However, more developed societies 
eventually deplete the tribes' resources, and/or teach them the
more attractive lifestyle.   Different sinularities will occur
to different scientific disciplines and industries at different
times.  

We've heard speculation about most people getting left behind by
the few very lucky and/or very skilled posthumans who got there first.
My speculation is that most people will have a chance to hop on board 
the new bandwagons.  Most who really want it will
get it.  Who gets it will be based more on motivation and values
than innate intelligence, since intelligence will be quite enhanceable, 
and it will probably be easier to improve subnormal intelligence than expand
into the frontiers of superior intelligence (that's also a far
greater moral imperative for non-Extropian society).  It's much
more difficult to enhance motivations and values, since it's
not always clear which ones will be the best for doing well in various
singularities.   Oftentimes, we find that some culture has 
conquered something similar before, and has embedded the techniques
deep inside some logically deep ritual with a relation to the
singularity that's only obscure at best.  Such memetic conservatives
might fair better those who strike out memetically in a semi-random
fashion, or in a ponderous logical fashion well-suited to hard
science but ill-suited to prohibitively complex social phenomenon
and human behavior (esp. one's own).

Who will be prepared for Future Rapture -- the Great Uploading, that is!

