From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Vinge's Technological Singularity

Nick Szabo:

> Furthermore, designing intelligence is far more uncertain than 
> extrapolating improvements in hardware (either
> via trendline or via theoretical designs, eg Drexler's nanomachines).
> If we can predict the precise effects of a piece of software, we've
> already written it.  Predicting and writing software are nearly
> the same thing.

I largely agree with this; however I personally think that AI is
proceeding right on "schedule", maybe even better than I for
one would think.  I am very optimistic about the prospects for
AI, and I am one who "blames" the apparent glacial progress on
the lack of widely available computer power.

I look at it this way:  to me it's unreasonable to expect our
progress on AI software to vastly outstrip the machines in
wide use for developing that software, and I measure it roughly
by brain equivalence, on the assumption that selection pressures
and natural evolution won't come up with solutions that are vastly
wasteful (of course in all of this there's a few orders of magnitude
allowed for slop).

So it seems to me that, roughly, progress in AI software (information
processing, adaptability, behavioral complexity) is "on track" if
it produces systems with capabilities comparable to biological
organisms with similar processing power as the machines on which
the (real-time) AI systems are developed.  Unfortunately, it's
difficult to make such comparisons in a rigorous way, but I think
it's okay to wave hands a bit.

I will use, for the purposes of example, a figure of one FLOP per
synapse per "update" and a rate of 100 updates per second.  If
anything, this seems likely to be an underestimate of the effective
processing capabilities of nervous systems.  This allows us to make
a crude correspondence between organisms and computers:  examples:

Nematode: about 1E06 FLOP/sec
Housefly: about 1E10 FLOP/sec
Mouse:    about 1E13 FLOP/sec
Human:    about 1E16 FLOP/sec

Currently, one can purchase a specialized box of i860 processors
capable of 1E09 FLOP/sec for about $50,000.  So to my thinking,
AI software is about on track if it offers the information processing
abilities, adaptability, and behavioral complexity of a housefly
when we reach the next order of magnitude of increase in widely-available
processing power.

The admittedly crude and preliminary theorizing and experimentation
that AI has produced so far strikes me as being at least as far along
as it needs to be to keep up with the hardware.  As Nick says, it's
impossible to know whether that will continue, but I'm pretty optimistic
about it.

Avenues that I think are particularly promising for the rest of the
century:

Edelman's Theory of Neuronal Group Selection
Genetic Programming
Implementing the Society of Mind
Subsumption-style modular behaviorism
Regression and correlation in complex recurrent neural net architectures

It is difficult right now to even experiment with these kinds of
systems because the tiniest experiments take forever to run; rather
than thinking that this shows some fundamental flaw in our current
theories, to me it's more a reflection of the pitiful available
resources.  How are we supposed to, for example, study the processing
capabilities of housefly-size neural nets when each little tweak
would take weeks of computation on the Sun workstation sitting on
the typical researcher's desk?

This paucity of computing resources is what caused me to temporarily
put my own AI research on hold for now, to let me play with applications
where the technology is closer to the vision.

Uploading is a completely different issue, and I have to admit that
although I'm optimistic about the long-term prospects, I think
we're way behind here; in terms of technology, we should have
working computer models of that nematode, but we don't.  The reasons
for this (IMO):

* simulating the complex environmental feedback provided to an organism
  is very expensive

* Simulating the musculature, metabolism, etc of an organism is expensive

* We don't know how neurons work; every refinement of neuron models
  seems to add more and more computing requirements to the upload
  estimate.  Unfortunately, for uploading we can't just use simplifying
  models and declare victory unless the uploaded organism behaves
  very much like the physical organism it attempts to model.  We don't
  even have adequate metrics to determine when this is in fact the case.

* It's nearly impossible to map out an organism's nervous system.  This
  has been done, to an extent, for the nematode (see, e.g. "Motor
  Behavior and Motor Nervous System Function in the Nematode Ascaris
  Suum", Stretton et. al., J. Parasitology 78(2) 1992 pp206-214), but
  connectivity and crude shape are not enough; neurons are individual
  organisms and their behavior depends on lots of internal detail that
  is extremely difficult to determine.  Without substantial advances
  in techniques for mapping nervous systems, I can't see how we can
  even hope to upload a housfly and its ~ 100ish million synapses.
  There's some hope in the thought that, contrary to the nematode case,
  much can probably be done with approximate connectivity since exact
  connectivity once we get to the level of houseflys and beyond is
  likely largely epigenetic -- but even so I consider our inability to
  map nervous systems to be the most important problem in upload
  science; more important even than neuron modelling.

derek
